onejos, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.dc7dc32149559b6690b7b7764bf9f5250586db02.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.dc7dc32149559b6690b7b7764bf9f5250586db02.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued November 1 at 4:11PM MST until November 2 at 4:15PM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding of area rivers and streams caused by rain and\nsnowmelt is expected.\n\n* WHERE...Western Conejos County in south central Colorado...\n\n* WHEN...Until 415 PM MST Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is imminent or occurring.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 410 PM MST, Rain and snowmelt is causing flooding. Between 1\nand 2 inches of rain have fallen.\n- Additional rainfall amounts of 2 to 3 inches are possible in the\nwarned area.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nPlatoro, Cumbres Pass, and areas along Highway 17.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T16:15:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Conejos, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6bdcad09d7929c655ae25a4a0c0235afc0975ee9.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6bdcad09d7929c655ae25a4a0c0235afc0975ee9.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued November 1 at 1:50PM MST until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Feet, Upper Rio\nGrande Valley and Eastern San Juan Mountains Below 10000 Feet.\n\n* WHEN...Through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone location.\nRockslides will be possible in the mountainous areas. Highway 160\nand Highway 17 will likely be significantly impacted by this storm.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Heavy rainfall is expected to fall through Monday across the\narea, leading to elevated concerns for flooding in areas that\nhave recently received excessive moisture, or have recently\nexperienced flooding.\n- The greatest risk of flooding will be along the Conejos River and\nthe South Fork of the Rio Grande, including South Fork and\nMogote.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Upper Rio Grande Valley/Eastern San Juan Mountains Below 10000 Ft; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6452f67ccada8c80a213b32735fb106e5b65d3ae.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6452f67ccada8c80a213b32735fb106e5b65d3ae.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued November 1 at 1:50PM MST until November 4 at 5:00AM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall will be possible,\nespecially over the western half of San Luis Valley.\n\n* WHERE...The western half of the San Luis Valley, including\nAntonito, Del Norte, Monte Vista and Alamosa.\n\n* WHEN...From 5 PM MST this afternoon through late Tuesday night.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Runoff from heavy rain and melting snow over the San Juan\nMountains may cause flooding in the San Luis Valley, especially\nMonday and Tuesday as high flows move out of the mountains and\nthrough the Rio Grande and Conejos River.\n- The greatest risk of flooding will be along the Conejos River and\nthe Rio Grande, including Del Norte, Monte Vista and Alamosa.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-04T05:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Del Norte Vicinity/Northern San Luis Valley Below 8500 Ft; Alamosa  Vicinity/Central San Luis Valley Below 8500 Ft; Southern San Luis Valley","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6452f67ccada8c80a213b32735fb106e5b65d3ae.002.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6452f67ccada8c80a213b32735fb106e5b65d3ae.002.1"},{"event":"Winter Storm Warning","headline":"Winter Storm Warning issued November 1 at 10:57AM MST until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Heavy snow expected. Total snow accumulations 8 to 14\ninches. Winds gusting as high as 50 mph.\n\n* WHERE...Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Feet.\n\n* WHEN...From 5 PM this afternoon to 11 AM MST Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Travel could be very difficult, especially tonight and\nMonday morning over Wolf Creek Pass and Cumbres Pass. Strong winds\ncould produce areas of blowing and drifting snow.","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.994b8191c6e7030d91cd62eb8345f4293ce0424b.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.994b8191c6e7030d91cd62eb8345f4293ce0424b.001.1"},{"event":"Avalanche Advisory","headline":"Avalanche Advisory issued October 31 at 7:16PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"SABCO\n\nTHE FOLLOWING MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at\nwww.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\nBeyer & Pritchett","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Northwestern San Juan M